# =============================================================================
# labwc shutdown — what runs as the window manager is exiting
# =============================================================================
# The mirror image of `autostart`. labwc runs this as a shell script when it is
# preparing to terminate, while WAYLAND_DISPLAY is still valid.
#
# All it does is undo the one thing autostart did: tell systemd the graphical
# session is over, so the portals, the wallpaper, the keyring agent and the
# shell stop with it instead of lingering as orphans into the next login.
#
# Orphans matter more here than they sound. A leftover portal from a previous
# session is one of the classic causes of "screen recording worked yesterday".
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠️ THIS FILE IS THE HEAD START, NOT THE CLEAN-UP.  (2026-09-04)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It used to be the whole of the clean-up, and it was not enough. Two reasons:
#
#   1. It only stops services. It does not remove the settings this session
#      wrote into your personal background manager — the note saying the screen
#      is called wayland-0, the note saying this desktop is called "Aquarius".
#      Those outlive the logout, and they are what made GNOME fail to start
#      afterwards. That was Royce's "it kicks me back to the login screen a
#      couple of times" bug.
#
#   2. labwc does not wait for this script. It starts it and carries on with
#      its own shutdown, so anything slow in here is racing the compositor's
#      exit and may simply be cut off.
#
# The real clean-up is therefore in /usr/bin/aquarius-session, which now stays
# alive until the window manager has gone and then runs the whole sequence with
# a time limit on it — stop the services, wait, remove the settings, close
# anything left running. See /usr/libexec/aquarius-session-lib.
#
# This line stays because it is a genuinely useful head start: it lets systemd
# begin stopping things a moment earlier, while the screen still exists, which
# is the kindest moment for a program to be asked to close. Running it twice is
# harmless — stopping something already stopped is not an error.
# =============================================================================

systemctl --user stop graphical-session.target 2>/dev/null || true
